</div>
<div>
<div class="faq-list"><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>What room size should I buy for?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Buy for the room where you will actually use the purifier most, then leave a margin. A purifier that only works well on its loudest setting is usually undersized in real life.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Do I need a bigger purifier than I think?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Often, yes. Manufacturer room-size claims can be optimistic, especially if you want quieter operation. A stronger purifier on a lower setting can be easier to live with.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is a more expensive purifier automatically better?</summary><p class="faq-answer">No. Bigger or pricier models can be worthwhile for large spaces, but airflow, filter cost, noise, and reliability matter more than price alone.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Does quiet mode mean it is still cleaning effectively?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Not always. Very quiet modes may move too little air to make much difference. The useful question is whether the purifier cleans well at a noise level you can tolerate daily.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>How much do filter costs matter?</summary><p class="faq-answer">A lot. Filter replacement is the main long-term cost, so a cheaper purifier can become less attractive if filters are expensive or hard to find.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Should I just rely on auto mode?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Auto mode is convenient, but most people should still understand the fan settings. During smoke, cooking smells, dust, or allergy spikes, manually increasing the fan can help.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Where should I place an air purifier?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Place it where air can move freely around the intake and output. Corners, curtains, and blocked vents reduce performance.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Do air purifiers remove odors?</summary><p class="faq-answer">They can help with some odors if they include a useful carbon filter, but odor control is not the same as particle filtration. Ventilation and source control still matter.</p></details>
</div>
